 Grade the Sky port against wasm/sky.zig.

 Seam 1 on a clock. Every function is a pure function of the STEP, so both sides
 walk the same table of steps in the same order.

 THE TWO COLOUR STREAMS ARE GRADED EXACTLY, as integers, with no tolerance
 anywhere near them. That is the whole point of this module: a colour one off is a
 wrong colour, not a rounding difference, and it is also the first thing in this
 port that could not be expressed at all before the plug learned real-to-int and
 real-from-int. skyColor rounds a lerped channel and packs three of them; neither
 half was reachable. See PORTING_NOTES A1 and plugs-backlog 2.03.

 An exact gate over a ROUNDED quantity is a real bet, and it is worth saying what
 is being bet. The port computes the channel in f64 and the game in f32, so a
 channel that lands near a half could round to different integers on the two sides
 and the colour would differ by one. That is not a tolerance to widen -- it would
 be a genuine disagreement about a pixel's colour, and the right response would be
 to say so, not to hide it. Fifteen steps across the whole clock are graded and
 none of them lands there.

 THE STEPS ARE THE CLOCK'S SHAPE, NOT COVERAGE: step 0, the squared ramp while the
 sun is high, the disc's centre exactly on the horizon where the warmth peaks at
 0.99994, the moment it is FULLY SET -- which is the branch boundary inside
 sun-set-fraction, and the table straddles it by four hundredths of a pixel -- and
 well into night where both fractions clamp and stay clamped.

 The placement is graded over eight headings crossing the visibility limit in both
 directions, at two focals, so `visible` answers False as often as True.

 SUN HEIGHT TAKES THE SCREEN-COORDINATE GATE, and that is not a loosening -- it is
 the same cancellation D3 records, in its most extreme instance yet. A height is
 244 minus a term of nearly the same size, so the disc's centre ON the horizon is
 a difference of two large numbers: 244 - 243.993609 = 0.006391. Measured there,
 the f32-against-f64 gap is 2.6e-6 PIXELS in absolute terms -- nothing -- and
 4.1e-4 in relative terms, four hundred times a 1e-6 relative gate, because
 cancellation amplifies by the 38,180 between 244 and 0.0064. The tree's horizon
 case amplified by fifteen; this is the same defect of a purely relative gate,
 three orders of magnitude worse.

 These heights ARE screen pixels -- the module says so, they compare directly
 against the mountain silhouettes -- so the mixed gate that grades every other
 screen coordinate in this port is the one that belongs here.

 ITS FLOOR IS 1e-4 AND NOT D3's 1e-3, because the floor is CALIBRATED to what it
 has to admit and nothing more. Here that is one value, the 2.6e-6 at the horizon
 crossing, so a 1e-3 floor would sit four hundred times above the gap it exists
 for -- and it showed: perturbing sun-start-px by 2e-4 left this seam green while
 two others reddened. At 1e-4 it admits the cancellation with forty times to spare
 and catches that perturbation. Verified both ways. Everything else on
 the clock is a fraction in 0..1 with no cancellation in it, and stays at 1e-6
 relative.

 We say:

Section: The Clock

  steps : List Real
  steps = [0.0, 250.0, 500.0, 1000.0, 2000.0, 3000.0, 3500.0, 3983.0, 4000.0, 4400.0, 4734.0, 4800.0, 5200.0, 6000.0, 8000.0]


Section: The Placement

 Eight headings at two focals, the probe's order: every heading at the base focal,
 then every heading at the pulled-in one.

  headings : List Real
  headings = [0.0 - 2.2176, 0.0 - 1.6, 0.0 - 1.0, 0.0, 1.0, 2.0, 3.0, 0.0 - 3.0]

  probe-step : Real
  probe-step = 3000.0

  poses-at : Real, List Real, Integer -> List SunPos
  poses-at (cf) (hs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length hs then []
    else [sun-pos (list-at hs i) probe-step cf camera-w] & poses-at cf hs (i + 1)

  all-poses : List SunPos
  all-poses = (poses-at focal headings 0) & poses-at (focal * 0.6) headings 0

  pose-visible : List SunPos, Integer -> List Boolean
  pose-visible (ps) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ps then []
    else [(list-at ps i).visible] & pose-visible ps (i + 1)

  pose-x : List SunPos, Integer -> List Real
  pose-x (ps) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ps then []
    else [(list-at ps i).x] & pose-x ps (i + 1)

  pose-y : List SunPos, Integer -> List Real
  pose-y (ps) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ps then []
    else [(list-at ps i).y] & pose-y ps (i + 1)

  pose-scale : List SunPos, Integer -> List Real
  pose-scale (ps) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ps then []
    else [(list-at ps i).scale] & pose-scale ps (i + 1)

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni (grade-px   "sky-height  " (list-map sun-height-px steps) g-sky-height 0.0001 0.000001)
    print-line-uni (grade-rel  "sky-dusk    " (list-map sun-set-fraction steps) g-sky-dusk 0.000001)
    print-line-uni (grade-rel  "sky-warmth  " (list-map sunset-warmth steps) g-sky-warmth 0.000001)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints "sky-color   " (list-map sky-color steps) g-sky-color)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints "sky-horizon " (list-map horizon-color steps) g-sky-horizon)
    print-line-uni (grade-bools "sun-visible" (pose-visible all-poses 0) g-sun-visible)
    print-line-uni (grade-px   "sun-x       " (pose-x all-poses 0) g-sun-x 0.001 0.000001)
    print-line-uni (grade-px   "sun-y       " (pose-y all-poses 0) g-sun-y 0.001 0.000001)
    print-line-uni (grade-rel  "sun-scale   " (pose-scale all-poses 0) g-sun-scale 0.000001)
  end
